   "source": [
    "# Hito 4 — A/B Testing Framework\n",
    "## eSIM Pricing Engine · `esim-pricing-engine`\n",
    "\n",
    "---\n",
    "\n",
    "### 🗺️ Business Framing\n",
    "\n",
    "The elasticity model (Hito 2) tells us the *expected* effect of a price change. But expectations are estimated from observational data — they carry uncertainty. Before rolling out a new price to 100% of traffic, we need to **validate the recommendation experimentally**.\n",
    "\n",
    "A pricing A/B test answers: *Is the CR difference we observe after changing price real, or just noise?*\n",
    "\n",
    "This hito builds a **reusable experimentation framework** with three components:\n",
    "\n",
    "| Component | Question answered | When used |\n",
    "|-----------|------------------|-----------|\n",
    "| **Power analysis** | How many sessions do we need? How long to run? | Before the experiment |\n",
    "| **Frequentist evaluator** | Is the result statistically significant? | After the experiment |\n",
    "| **Bayesian evaluator** | What's the probability the new price is better? | After the experiment |\n",
    "\n",
    "**Why both frequentist and Bayesian?**  \n",
    "Frequentist tests are the industry default and easy to explain to stakeholders. Bayesian tests are more actionable — instead of \"p=0.03, reject H₀\", you get \"94% probability the new price has higher CR\". For a pricing team making continuous decisions, probability statements are more useful than binary reject/fail-to-reject.\n",
    "\n",
    "**Scenario for this notebook:**  \n",
    "Our elasticity model suggests `europe_leisure` is overpriced. We want to test a **−10% price reduction** (hypothesis: this raises CR enough to improve margin per session). We'll design the experiment, simulate its outcome, and evaluate it with both frameworks."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "import sys\n",
    "sys.path.insert(0, \"..\")\n",
    "\n",
    "import warnings\n",
    "warnings.filterwarnings(\"ignore\")\n",
    "\n",
    "import numpy as np\n",
    "import pandas as pd\n",
    "import matplotlib.pyplot as plt\n",
    "import matplotlib.patches as mpatches\n",
    "import seaborn as sns\n",
    "from scipy import stats\n",
    "from pathlib import Path\n",
    "\n",
    "from src.ab_testing import (\n",
    "    sample_size_per_variant,\n",
    "    experiment_duration_days,\n",
    "    mde_from_sample_size,\n",
    "    design_summary,\n",
    "    frequentist_test,\n",
    "    bayesian_test,\n",
    "    simulate_experiment,\n",
    "    simulate_peeking,\n",
    ")\n",
    "\n",
    "sns.set_theme(style=\"whitegrid\", palette=\"muted\", font_scale=1.1)\n",
    "plt.rcParams[\"figure.dpi\"] = 120\n",
    "plt.rcParams[\"axes.spines.top\"]   = False\n",
    "plt.rcParams[\"axes.spines.right\"] = False\n",
    "\n",
    "# Experiment parameters — europe_leisure representative destination\n",
    "BASELINE_CR      = 0.056    # 5.6% observed CR\n",
    "DAILY_SESSIONS   = 120      # avg daily sessions for the destination\n",
    "PRICE_CHANGE     = -0.10    # −10% price test\n",
    "TRUE_CR_LIFT     = 0.18     # true effect: +18% CR (from elasticity: β=−2.14, −10% price → +21.4% CR)\n",
    "\n",
    "print(f\"Experiment scenario: europe_leisure, −10% price test\")\n",
    "print(f\"Baseline CR:         {BASELINE_CR:.1%}\")\n",
    "print(f\"Daily sessions:      {DAILY_SESSIONS}\")\n",
    "print(f\"Hypothesised CR lift: +{TRUE_CR_LIFT:.0%} (from elasticity model)\")\n",
    "print(f\"Expected treatment CR: {BASELINE_CR*(1+TRUE_CR_LIFT):.1%}\")"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## 1. Experiment Design: Power Analysis\n",
    "\n",
    "Power analysis answers: *given our baseline CR and the effect we want to detect, how many sessions do we need per variant?*\n",
    "\n",
    "Key parameters:\n",
    "- **α = 0.05** — we accept a 5% probability of a false positive (declaring a winner when there isn't one)\n",
    "- **Power = 0.80** — we accept a 20% probability of missing a real effect\n",
    "- **MDE** — minimum detectable effect: the smallest CR lift worth detecting"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "# Design summary table across a range of MDE values\n",
    "summary = design_summary(\n",
    "    baseline_cr    = BASELINE_CR,\n",
    "    daily_sessions = DAILY_SESSIONS,\n",
    "    alpha          = 0.05,\n",
    "    power          = 0.80,\n",
    ")\n",
    "\n",
    "summary.style.format({\n",
    "    \"mde_relative\":   \"{:.0%}\",\n",
    "    \"mde_absolute\":   \"{:.4f}\",\n",
    "    \"n_per_variant\":  \"{:,}\",\n",
    "    \"total_sessions\": \"{:,}\",\n",
    "    \"duration_days\":  \"{:,}d\",\n",
    "    \"duration_weeks\": \"{:.1f}w\",\n",
    "}).background_gradient(subset=[\"duration_days\"], cmap=\"RdYlGn_r\")"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "# For our specific experiment: detect +10% CR lift (conservative — true effect is +18%)\n",
    "MDE          = 0.10\n",
    "n_required   = sample_size_per_variant(BASELINE_CR, MDE)\n",
    "duration     = experiment_duration_days(n_required, DAILY_SESSIONS)\n",
    "\n",
    "print(f\"Experiment design (MDE = {MDE:.0%} relative CR lift):\")\n",
    "print(f\"  Sessions per variant:  {n_required:,}\")\n",
    "print(f\"  Total sessions needed: {n_required*2:,}\")\n",
    "print(f\"  Duration:              {duration} days ({duration/7:.1f} weeks)\")\n",
    "print(f\"  At {DAILY_SESSIONS} sessions/day, {duration} days is feasible.\")\n",
    "print()\n",
    "print(f\"Since our hypothesised true lift is {TRUE_CR_LIFT:.0%} (> MDE of {MDE:.0%}),\")\n",
    "print(f\"we are well-powered to detect this effect — actual power will be > 80%.\")"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "# Visualise the precision/duration trade-off\n",
    "mde_range   = np.linspace(0.05, 0.40, 100)\n",
    "n_values    = [sample_size_per_variant(BASELINE_CR, m) for m in mde_range]\n",
    "day_values  = [experiment_duration_days(n, DAILY_SESSIONS) for n in n_values]\n",
    "\n",
    "fig, axes = plt.subplots(1, 2, figsize=(12, 4))\n",
    "\n",
    "axes[0].plot(mde_range * 100, n_values, color=\"#2E86AB\", lw=2)\n",
    "axes[0].axvline(MDE * 100, color=\"#C73E1D\", lw=1.5, linestyle=\"--\",\n",
    "                label=f\"Our MDE = {MDE:.0%}\")\n",
    "axes[0].axhline(n_required, color=\"#C73E1D\", lw=1, linestyle=\":\")\n",
    "axes[0].scatter([MDE*100], [n_required], color=\"#C73E1D\", s=80, zorder=5)\n",
    "axes[0].set_xlabel(\"MDE (% relative CR lift)\")\n",
    "axes[0].set_ylabel(\"Sessions per Variant\")\n",
    "axes[0].set_title(\"Sample Size Required vs MDE\")\n",
    "axes[0].legend()\n",
    "\n",
    "axes[1].plot(mde_range * 100, day_values, color=\"#A23B72\", lw=2)\n",
    "axes[1].axvline(MDE * 100, color=\"#C73E1D\", lw=1.5, linestyle=\"--\",\n",
    "                label=f\"Our MDE = {MDE:.0%} → {duration}d\")\n",
    "axes[1].axhline(duration, color=\"#C73E1D\", lw=1, linestyle=\":\")\n",
    "axes[1].scatter([MDE*100], [duration], color=\"#C73E1D\", s=80, zorder=5)\n",
    "axes[1].axhline(30, color=\"grey\", lw=1, linestyle=\":\", alpha=0.6, label=\"30-day budget\")\n",
    "axes[1].set_xlabel(\"MDE (% relative CR lift)\")\n",
    "axes[1].set_ylabel(\"Experiment Duration (days)\")\n",
    "axes[1].set_title(f\"Duration vs MDE  ({DAILY_SESSIONS} sessions/day)\")\n",
    "axes[1].legend()\n",
    "\n",
    "plt.suptitle(\"Power Analysis: Precision–Duration Trade-off\", fontsize=12, fontweight=\"bold\")\n",
    "plt.tight_layout()\n",
    "plt.show()"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## 2. Simulate the Experiment\n",
    "\n",
    "We simulate a completed experiment: sessions are drawn from a Binomial distribution with the true CR for each variant. This is realistic — in practice you'd collect these counts from your analytics platform."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "exp_data, ground_truth = simulate_experiment(\n",
    "    baseline_cr        = BASELINE_CR,\n",
    "    true_lift_relative = TRUE_CR_LIFT,\n",
    "    n_per_variant      = n_required,\n",
    "    daily_sessions     = DAILY_SESSIONS,\n",
    "    seed               = 42,\n",
    ")\n",
    "\n",
    "print(\"=\" * 50)\n",
    "print(\"SIMULATED EXPERIMENT RESULTS\")\n",
    "print(\"=\" * 50)\n",
    "print(f\"Control   (current price):  {exp_data['n_control']:,} sessions, \"\n",
    "      f\"{exp_data['conversions_control']:,} conversions  \"\n",
    "      f\"→ CR = {exp_data['observed_cr_control']:.2%}\")\n",
    "print(f\"Treatment (−10% price):     {exp_data['n_treatment']:,} sessions, \"\n",
    "      f\"{exp_data['conversions_treatment']:,} conversions  \"\n",
    "      f\"→ CR = {exp_data['observed_cr_treatment']:.2%}\")\n",
    "print()\n",
    "print(f\"True CR lift (ground truth): +{ground_truth['true_lift_relative']:.0%}\")\n",
    "print(f\"Observed CR lift:            +{(exp_data['observed_cr_treatment']/exp_data['observed_cr_control']-1):.1%}\")"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## 3. Frequentist Evaluation\n",
    "\n",
    "Two-proportion z-test. The key outputs:\n",
    "- **p-value**: probability of observing this CR difference (or larger) if the null hypothesis were true\n",
    "- **95% CI on the difference**: range of plausible true effect sizes\n",
    "- **Significant at α=0.05?**: binary decision"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "freq_result = frequentist_test(\n",
    "    n_control            = exp_data[\"n_control\"],\n",
    "    conversions_control  = exp_data[\"conversions_control\"],\n",
    "    n_treatment          = exp_data[\"n_treatment\"],\n",
    "    conversions_treatment= exp_data[\"conversions_treatment\"],\n",
    ")\n",
    "\n",
    "print(\"=\" * 50)\n",
    "print(\"FREQUENTIST TEST RESULTS\")\n",
    "print(\"=\" * 50)\n",
    "print(f\"CR Control:        {freq_result['cr_control']:.2%}\")\n",
    "print(f\"CR Treatment:      {freq_result['cr_treatment']:.2%}\")\n",
    "print(f\"Absolute lift:     {freq_result['absolute_lift']:+.4f}\")\n",
    "print(f\"Relative lift:     {freq_result['relative_lift']:+.1%}\")\n",
    "print(f\"Z-statistic:       {freq_result['z_stat']:.3f}\")\n",
    "print(f\"P-value:           {freq_result['p_value']:.4f}\")\n",
    "print(f\"95% CI (diff):     [{freq_result['ci_95_lo']:+.4f}, {freq_result['ci_95_hi']:+.4f}]\")\n",
    "print()\n",
    "verdict = \"✅ SIGNIFICANT\" if freq_result[\"significant\"] else \"❌ NOT SIGNIFICANT\"\n",
    "print(f\"Verdict (α=0.05):  {verdict}\")"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## 4. Bayesian Evaluation\n",
    "\n",
    "The Beta-Binomial conjugate model. We start with a flat (uniform) prior over CR — equivalent to saying \"before seeing any data, any CR between 0% and 100% is equally plausible\". After observing the data, we update to a posterior distribution.\n",
    "\n",
    "The key output is **P(treatment > control)** — a direct probability statement about which variant is better. No p-value interpretation required."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "bayes_result = bayesian_test(\n",
    "    n_control            = exp_data[\"n_control\"],\n",
    "    conversions_control  = exp_data[\"conversions_control\"],\n",
    "    n_treatment          = exp_data[\"n_treatment\"],\n",
    "    conversions_treatment= exp_data[\"conversions_treatment\"],\n",
    ")\n",
    "\n",
    "print(\"=\" * 50)\n",
    "print(\"BAYESIAN TEST RESULTS\")\n",
    "print(\"=\" * 50)\n",
    "print(f\"Posterior mean CR (control):   {bayes_result['posterior_mean_control']:.2%}\")\n",
    "print(f\"Posterior mean CR (treatment): {bayes_result['posterior_mean_treatment']:.2%}\")\n",
    "print()\n",
    "print(f\"P(treatment > control):        {bayes_result['prob_treatment_better']:.1%}\")\n",
    "print(f\"P(practically equivalent):     {bayes_result['prob_rope']:.1%}\")\n",
    "print(f\"95% Credible Interval (diff):  [{bayes_result['ci_95_lo']:+.4f}, {bayes_result['ci_95_hi']:+.4f}]\")\n",
    "print()\n",
    "print(f\"Expected loss if we launch treatment:  {bayes_result['expected_loss_if_launch']:.5f}\")\n",
    "print(f\"Expected loss if we roll back:         {bayes_result['expected_loss_if_rollback']:.5f}\")\n",
    "print()\n",
    "launch = bayes_result['expected_loss_if_launch'] < bayes_result['expected_loss_if_rollback']\n",
    "print(f\"Decision (min expected loss): {'✅ LAUNCH TREATMENT' if launch else '❌ KEEP CONTROL'}\")"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "# Visualise posterior distributions\n",
    "samples_a = bayes_result[\"posterior_samples_a\"]\n",
    "samples_b = bayes_result[\"posterior_samples_b\"]\n",
    "diff      = bayes_result[\"diff_samples\"]\n",
    "\n",
    "fig, axes = plt.subplots(1, 2, figsize=(13, 4))\n",
    "\n",
    "# Panel 1: Posterior distributions of CR\n",
    "axes[0].hist(samples_a * 100, bins=80, alpha=0.6, color=\"#2E86AB\",\n",
    "             density=True, label=f\"Control (current price)\")\n",
    "axes[0].hist(samples_b * 100, bins=80, alpha=0.6, color=\"#C73E1D\",\n",
    "             density=True, label=f\"Treatment (−10% price)\")\n",
    "axes[0].axvline(bayes_result[\"posterior_mean_control\"]  * 100, color=\"#2E86AB\", lw=2, linestyle=\"--\")\n",
    "axes[0].axvline(bayes_result[\"posterior_mean_treatment\"] * 100, color=\"#C73E1D\", lw=2, linestyle=\"--\")\n",
    "axes[0].set_xlabel(\"Conversion Rate (%)\")\n",
    "axes[0].set_ylabel(\"Posterior Density\")\n",
    "axes[0].set_title(\"Posterior Distributions of CR\\n(control vs treatment)\")\n",
    "axes[0].legend()\n",
    "\n",
    "# Panel 2: Posterior distribution of the difference\n",
    "prob_positive = np.mean(diff > 0)\n",
    "axes[1].hist(diff * 100, bins=80, color=\"#A23B72\", alpha=0.8, density=True,\n",
    "             label=\"Posterior of CR difference\")\n",
    "axes[1].axvline(0, color=\"black\", lw=1.5, linestyle=\"--\", label=\"No effect\")\n",
    "axes[1].axvline(np.percentile(diff, 2.5)  * 100, color=\"grey\", lw=1, linestyle=\":\")\n",
    "axes[1].axvline(np.percentile(diff, 97.5) * 100, color=\"grey\", lw=1, linestyle=\":\",\n",
    "                label=\"95% Credible Interval\")\n",
    "\n",
    "# Shade positive region\n",
    "x_vals = np.linspace(diff.min(), diff.max(), 1000)\n",
    "kde     = stats.gaussian_kde(diff)\n",
    "y_vals  = kde(x_vals)\n",
    "axes[1].fill_between(x_vals * 100, y_vals,\n",
    "                     where=(x_vals > 0), alpha=0.25, color=\"#3BB273\",\n",
    "                     label=f\"P(treatment better) = {prob_positive:.1%}\")\n",
    "\n",
    "axes[1].set_xlabel(\"CR Difference: Treatment − Control (pp)\")\n",
    "axes[1].set_ylabel(\"Posterior Density\")\n",
    "axes[1].set_title(\"Posterior Distribution of CR Lift\")\n",
    "axes[1].legend(fontsize=8)\n",
    "\n",
    "plt.suptitle(\"Bayesian A/B Test — Posterior Inference\", fontsize=12, fontweight=\"bold\")\n",
    "plt.tight_layout()\n",
    "plt.show()"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## 5. Frequentist vs Bayesian — Side-by-Side Comparison\n",
    "\n",
    "Both frameworks should agree directionally. The difference is in *what they tell you* and *how you communicate it* to stakeholders."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "comparison = pd.DataFrame([\n",
    "    {\n",
    "        \"Framework\": \"Frequentist\",\n",
    "        \"Key metric\": f\"p = {freq_result['p_value']:.4f}\",\n",
    "        \"Decision rule\": f\"p < 0.05 → {'Reject H₀' if freq_result['significant'] else 'Fail to reject H₀'}\",\n",
    "        \"Result\": '✅ Significant' if freq_result['significant'] else '❌ Not significant',\n",
    "        \"Communicates\": \"Probability of data given H₀ is true\",\n",
    "        \"Limitation\": \"Binary output; no early stopping; no magnitude\",\n",
    "    },\n",
    "    {\n",
    "        \"Framework\": \"Bayesian\",\n",
    "        \"Key metric\": f\"P(B>A) = {bayes_result['prob_treatment_better']:.1%}\",\n",
    "        \"Decision rule\": f\"Min expected loss → {'Launch' if launch else 'Rollback'}\",\n",
    "        \"Result\": '✅ Launch treatment' if launch else '❌ Keep control',\n",
    "        \"Communicates\": \"Probability treatment is better\",\n",
    "        \"Limitation\": \"Prior-dependent (mild with large n)\",\n",
    "    },\n",
    "])\n",
    "comparison.set_index(\"Framework\").T"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## 6. The Peeking Problem — Why Sequential Testing Matters\n",
    "\n",
    "A common mistake: checking significance every day and stopping the moment p < 0.05. This inflates the Type I error rate far above the nominal α=0.05. We demonstrate this empirically — a critical section for any DS claiming to understand experimentation rigor."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "print(\"Simulating peeking problem (1,000 experiments under null hypothesis)...\")\n",
    "print(\"(true_lift=0 means no real effect — any 'significant' result is a false positive)\")\n",
    "print()\n",
    "\n",
    "peeking_results = simulate_peeking(\n",
    "    baseline_cr    = BASELINE_CR,\n",
    "    n_total        = n_required,\n",
    "    true_lift      = 0.0,    # null: no real effect\n",
    "    n_simulations  = 1000,\n",
    "    alpha          = 0.05,\n",
    "    seed           = 42,\n",
    ")\n",
    "\n",
    "fpr_peeking = peeking_results[\"peeked_significant\"].mean()\n",
    "fpr_final   = peeking_results[\"final_significant\"].mean()\n",
    "\n",
    "print(f\"False positive rate (check only at end):      {fpr_final:.1%}  ← should be ~5%\")\n",
    "print(f\"False positive rate (peek at every 10% mark): {fpr_peeking:.1%}  ← inflated!\")\n",
    "print()\n",
    "print(f\"Peeking inflates false positives by {fpr_peeking/fpr_final:.1f}×.\")\n",
    "print(\"Solution: use sequential testing (e.g. always-valid p-values) or Bayesian approach\")\n",
    "print(\"if you need to monitor experiments in real time.\")"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "fig, ax = plt.subplots(figsize=(8, 4))\n",
    "\n",
    "categories  = [\"Correct test\\n(check at end)\", \"Peeking\\n(check at every 10%)\", \"Nominal α\"]\n",
    "fpr_values  = [fpr_final * 100, fpr_peeking * 100, 5.0]\n",
    "bar_colors  = [\"#2E86AB\", \"#C73E1D\", \"#F18F01\"]\n",
    "\n",
    "bars = ax.bar(categories, fpr_values, color=bar_colors, alpha=0.85, width=0.5)\n",
    "ax.axhline(5, color=\"black\", lw=1.5, linestyle=\"--\", label=\"Nominal α = 5%\")\n",
    "\n",
    "for bar, val in zip(bars, fpr_values):\n",
    "    ax.text(bar.get_x() + bar.get_width()/2, bar.get_height() + 0.3,\n",
    "            f\"{val:.1f}%\", ha=\"center\", va=\"bottom\", fontweight=\"bold\")\n",
    "\n",
    "ax.set_ylabel(\"False Positive Rate (%)\")\n",
    "ax.set_title(\"The Peeking Problem\\n(1,000 simulations under null hypothesis, no real effect)\",\n",
    "             fontsize=11)\n",
    "ax.set_ylim(0, max(fpr_values) * 1.25)\n",
    "ax.legend()\n",
    "plt.tight_layout()\n",
    "plt.show()"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## 7. MDE Calculator — Practical Decision Tool\n",
    "\n",
    "Given our traffic constraints, what's the smallest effect we can reliably detect? This is the question a commercial team asks when they say *\"how long do we need to run this?\"*"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "# MDE achievable in 14, 21, 30 days for different daily session volumes\n",
    "session_volumes = [50, 100, 150, 200, 300]\n",
    "day_budgets     = [14, 21, 30]\n",
    "\n",
    "rows = []\n",
    "for days in day_budgets:\n",
    "    for sessions in session_volumes:\n",
    "        n_available = int(sessions * days / 2)   # 50% traffic split, per variant\n",
    "        mde = mde_from_sample_size(n_available, BASELINE_CR) * 100\n",
    "        rows.append({\"days\": days, \"daily_sessions\": sessions, \"mde_pct\": round(mde, 1)})\n",
    "\n",
    "mde_table = pd.DataFrame(rows).pivot(index=\"daily_sessions\", columns=\"days\", values=\"mde_pct\")\n",
    "mde_table.columns.name = \"Experiment duration (days)\"\n",
    "mde_table.index.name   = \"Daily sessions\"\n",
    "\n",
    "print(\"Minimum Detectable Effect (% relative CR lift) by traffic and duration:\")\n",
    "print(\"(baseline CR = {:.1%}, α=0.05, power=80%)\".format(BASELINE_CR))\n",
    "print()\n",
    "mde_table.style.format(\"{:.1f}%\").background_gradient(cmap=\"RdYlGn_r\")"
